hypotheses.statement:
The quadratic moment $\kappa$ of the physical bounded-profile generators satisfies $$\label{D:order-zero}
 \mathbf1_{(s,\infty)}(\kappa)
 \mathbf1_{[0,s]}(h_0)\big|_l=0\qquad(s\geq0).$$

Let $H(x,y_1,\ldots,y_m)$, $m\ge1$, be a tempered, jointly translation-invariant distribution with $\Box_xH=0$ and $$\label{C:H-support}
 \mathop{\mathrm{supp}}_x H\subset C_y\cup K_y,\qquad
 C_y=\bigcup_iJ(y_i),\qquad
 K_y=\bigcap_{n\ {\mathrm{future\ null}}}
 \{x:\min_i n\cdot y_i\le n\cdot x\le\max_i n\cdot y_i\}.$$

Suppose that, near a real point $w_0$, a hyperfunction $u$ has a compact analytic-functional representative such that $$\label{R:FBI-polynomial}
 |\mathcal T_\Lambda u(w,\eta)|\leq C(1+\Lambda)^N
 \quad(w\in U, |\eta|=r_*,\ \Lambda\geq1)$$ for some neighborhood $U$ of $w_0$ and some $r_*>0$.

outputs.exact_statement:
If $g$ is bounded and smooth with bounded Hessian, then $$\label{F:taylor-equation}
 |h_g-g(Y)|\le C h,\qquad
 C=\tfrac12\sup_y\left\lVert g''(y)\right\rVert.$$ In particular $\left\lVert(h_g-g(Y))\psi\right\rVert\le C\left\lVert h\psi\right\rVert$ for $\psi\in\mathop{\mathrm{Dom}}h$.

On any scalar copy from <a href="#F:comparison-table" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="F:comparison-table">[F:comparison-table]</a>, with its embedding into the ambient internal space understood, one has $$\label{F:spectral-equation}
 \mathbf1_{(\lambda,\infty)}(h)
       \mathbf1_{[0,\lambda]}(h_\nu)=0,\qquad
 \left\lVert h^s\psi\right\rVert\le\left\lVert h_\nu^s\psi\right\rVert\quad(s>0).$$ The norm assertion includes the corresponding inclusion of domains. It does not assert that $h$ leaves the scalar copy invariant. For bounded profiles $f\le g$ one also has $$\label{F:bounded-spectral-order}
 \mathbf1_{(\lambda,\infty)}(h_f)
       \mathbf1_{(-\infty,\lambda]}(h_g)=0.$$

Let $g$ be bounded and smooth with Lipschitz constant $C_1$, and write $H_g(-\delta)=e^{-i\delta a}H_g e^{i\delta a}$. For $\delta,\lambda>0$, $$\label{F:normal-displacement}
 \pm\bigl(H_g-H_g(-\delta)\bigr)
 \le\delta\left(\frac a\lambda+\frac{\lambda C_1^2}2 b\right)$$ as forms on a joint momentum-smooth core. The commutator form with $a$ extends continuously to $\mathop{\mathrm{Dom}}\sqrt{1+a+b}$. If $\psi\in\bigcap_{m\ge1}\mathop{\mathrm{Dom}}(1+a+b)^m$, then $H_g\psi\in\mathop{\mathrm{Dom}}\sqrt a$.

On the $b>0$ representation there is a normalized positive operator-valued Borel measure $\mu$ on $\mathbb R^2$, with mutually commuting values on the internal multiplicity space, such that $$\label{N:measure-representation}
 H_g=b\int_{\mathbb R^2}g(Y+z)\,\mu(\mathrm dz),
 \qquad \mu(\mathbb R^2)=\mathbf 1.$$ Its values commute with $h$ and with the external variables $b,Y$. Its first moment is zero as a polarized integral on $\mathop{\mathrm{Dom}}h^{1/2}$. Its trace second moment is the increasing closed-form limit of compactly truncated moments and defines a positive self-adjoint operator $\kappa$ satisfying $$\label{N:moment-comparison}
 \int z\,\mu(\mathrm dz)=0,\qquad
 \kappa=\int |z|^2\,\mu(\mathrm dz)\leq h,
 \qquad K_n=b(|Y|^2+\kappa).$$ The second-moment inequality is a quadratic-form inequality and, since the operators strongly commute, has its usual joint spectral meaning. No assertion that $\mu$ is projection-valued, or that $\kappa=h$, is made.

There are continuous $L^2(M\,\mathrm dM)$-valued regularizations $\chi^n_{\rm reg}(r,M)$ of $$\label{D:chi}
 \chi^n(r,M)=(M^2+|r|^2)c_T^{(\bar n)}(M,r)$$ such that, with $w=q+r$ and $$\label{D:AB}
A_A=[\mu_n(q)\chi^n_{\rm reg}(r)]_{j_A^{(n)}},\qquad
B_A=[\mu_{\bar n}(r)\chi^{\bar n}_{\rm reg}(q)]_{j_A^{(\bar n)}},$$ one has $$\label{D:two-horizon}
 (M_o^2+|w|^2)A_z-(|w|^2-M_o^2)B_z+2M_ow_iB_i
 =P_q+P_r.$$ The degree of each polynomial is uniformly finite. The coefficients of $P_q$, polynomial in $q$, may depend on $r$; those of $P_r$, polynomial in $r$, may depend on $q$.

For $c=l,j_z,s_z$ there is $d_c\in\mathcal K$ such that, if $F\in C_c^\infty(0,R)$, then $$\label{D:tail}
 \mathcal U\bigl(c\mathcal H_0F\bigr)(\lambda)
 =\left(\int_0^\infty vF(v)\,\mathrm dv\right)\lambda^{-2}d_c
 \quad\text{for almost every }\lambda>R.$$ The label map is linear, and $$\label{D:tail-relations}
 d_{j_z}=-d_l,\qquad d_{s_z}=-d_l/3.$$

The quadratic moment $\kappa$ of the physical bounded-profile generators satisfies $$\label{D:order-zero}
 \mathbf1_{(s,\infty)}(\kappa)
 \mathbf1_{[0,s]}(h_0)\big|_l=0\qquad(s\geq0).$$

Let $X_0(W)$ be the scalar wedge real space in the $l$ channel, $\iota$ its embedding into the physical Hilbert space, and $X(W)$ the physical wedge real space. For $p(z)=c|z-d|^2+c_0$, $c>0$, put $$K_{0,p}=b\{c(|Y-d|^2+h_0)+c_0\},\qquad
 K_p=b\{c(|Y-d|^2+\kappa)+c_0\}.$$ Then $$\label{C:moment-localization}
 \iota e^{iK_{0,p}}X_0(W)\subset e^{iK_p}X(W).$$ If $g$ is bounded and smooth and $g\le p$, the left side also lies in $X(W_g)=e^{iH_g}X(W)$. Conversely, the actual parabolic real space satisfies $$\label{C:actual-parabolic-localization}
 X(W_p)\subset e^{iK_p}X(W).$$ These statements hold after translations of the null plane and reflection.

Let $H(x,y_1,\ldots,y_m)$, $m\ge1$, be a tempered, jointly translation-invariant distribution with $\Box_xH=0$ and $$\label{C:H-support}
 \mathop{\mathrm{supp}}_x H\subset C_y\cup K_y,\qquad
 C_y=\bigcup_iJ(y_i),\qquad
 K_y=\bigcap_{n\ {\mathrm{future\ null}}}
 \{x:\min_i n\cdot y_i\le n\cdot x\le\max_i n\cdot y_i\}.$$ For a compact transfer-momentum test $\varphi(k)$, let $H_\varphi(y)$ be its pairing with the partial Fourier transform in $x$, using $e^{-ikx}$. Suppose these pairings are holomorphic in the ordered spectator tube and, for $\mathop{\mathrm{supp}}\varphi\subset\{|k|\le K\}$, $0<K\le1$, obey $$\label{C:shifted-test-bound}
 |H_\varphi(y)|\le C p_N(\varphi)
 (1+|y|+\delta(y)^{-1})^N
 \exp(CK|\operatorname{Im}y|).$$ Here $p_N$ is one fixed finite Schwartz seminorm and $\delta(y)$ is the minimum distance of the successive imaginary gaps from the cone boundary ($\delta=1$ for $m=1$). Then $H$ is a finite sum $$H(x,y)=\sum_{i,P}C_{iP}(y)P(\partial_x)\Delta_0(x-y_i),$$ where the coefficients have tempered boundaries with the ordinary ordered spectator spectra. They are invariant under common spectator translation. The polynomials $P$ may be chosen independently modulo $k^2$. We normalize the massless commutator solution by $\Delta_0(0,\boldsymbol r)=0$ and $\partial_0\Delta_0(0,\boldsymbol r)=\delta(\boldsymbol r)$.

Let $Z$ be an even Hermitian compact polynomial vacuum vector, smooth under translations. For an invariant-mass spectral band $I\Subset(0,\infty)$ and $\Lambda>0$, $$\label{C:low-energy-bound}
 \|1_{P^0<\Lambda}E_I Z\|
 \le C_Z\Lambda\,\langle Z,P^0E_I Z\rangle^{1/2},$$ where the constant is independent of $I$ and $\Lambda$.

Let $B=-i\partial_\tau$ on $L^2(\mathbb R,\mathrm d\tau;\mathcal K)$, where $\mathcal K$ is any Hilbert space. Suppose $f\in\bigcap_m H^m(\mathbb R;\mathcal K)$ and $g(\tau)=e^{-ice^\tau}f(\tau)\in\mathop{\mathrm{Dom}}e^{-\pi B}$, with $c>0$. For every $r>0$ there is a finite integer $m$ such that $$\label{C:Hardy-power-bound}
 \|e^{r\tau}f\|\le C_{r,c}
 \bigl(\|f\|_{H^m}+\|e^{-\pi B}g\|\bigr).$$ The constants are uniform when $c$ varies in a compact subinterval of $(0,\infty)$.

With one consistent nonzero Ward normalization $c$, $$\begin{aligned}
 F_J(A)&=0,& A&=V,L,J,\label{P:J-flux}\\
 F^t_{\partial_b}(A)&=c\,\partial_b A\Omega,& A&=V,L,J.
 \label{P:translation-flux}
\end{aligned}$$ For primary targets $L,J$ and every conformal Killing field $X$, $$F_X^t(A)=c\,\delta_X A\Omega.
 \label{P:conformal-flux}$$ Here $\delta_X$ is the canonical differential action, including spin and weight. In particular, a special transformation centered at the target has zero flux, while centered dilation about $L$ gives $2cL\Omega$. With outward orientation in <a href="#P:flux-definition" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="P:flux-definition">[P:flux-definition]</a>, Wick time basis $C_0=ie$, the convention $i[P_\nu,A]=\partial_\nu A$, and upper-minus-lower order $At-tA$ in the cap difference, one has $c=-1$.

For compact real $r$-sets, compact strict merged $z$-sets, and compact sets of relative imaginary directions $v$ of the target order, there are $C,N<\infty$ such that $$\label{R:face-power}
 |W_2(r+i\varepsilon v;z)|\leq C\varepsilon^{-N},
 \qquad 0<\varepsilon\leq\varepsilon_0.$$ The same statement holds for every fixed finite derivative order. Consequently $W_2$ has an ordinary distributional $r$-boundary $F_0(r;z)$, holomorphic as a distribution-valued function on the strict merged tube.
